WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.304 --> 00:00:03.637
(Latin acoustic guitar)

00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:06.860
- Today I'm transforming into one

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.952
of Peru's most famous
huayno singers, Dina Paucar.

00:00:09.952 --> 00:00:13.140
One of El Salvador's most
celebrated artists, Tono Salazar.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.237
- I'll be playing Evita Peron.

00:00:14.237 --> 00:00:15.302
I think just growing up, she was always

00:00:15.302 --> 00:00:16.795
like this icon that women looked up to.

00:00:16.795 --> 00:00:18.104
She was fighting for labor rights,

00:00:18.104 --> 00:00:20.182
feminism before that
was really a movement,

00:00:20.182 --> 00:00:21.821
she had a charity and a foundation.

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:23.817
- He would make caricatures of people

00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:25.723
who were just dicks in history,

00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:27.893
kind of do commentary through his artwork.

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:29.187
He was sassy with his artwork,

00:00:29.187 --> 00:00:30.692
and I'm a little sassy with my words.

00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:32.271
- I grew up listening to Dina Paucar.

00:00:32.271 --> 00:00:34.296
My grandfather would play
it all the time in the car,

00:00:34.296 --> 00:00:36.042
her music has impacted me so much,

00:00:36.042 --> 00:00:38.026
because it reminds me
so much of my grandpa.

00:00:38.026 --> 00:00:41.544
Every time I listen to it,
I just get so emotional.

00:00:41.544 --> 00:00:43.718
- She was an icon, very powerful,

00:00:43.718 --> 00:00:45.074
and I'm very proud to tell my family

00:00:45.074 --> 00:00:48.021
that I got to play Evita Peron.

00:00:48.021 --> 00:00:50.081
- My hair's such a big
part of my identity,

00:00:50.081 --> 00:00:52.733
I felt like a different person.

00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:54.643
- My grandpa would have loved

00:00:54.643 --> 00:00:57.717
to have seen me transformed into Dina.

00:00:57.717 --> 00:00:59.665
- He was kind of pushing
for rights for all people,

00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:02.832
like I'm doing that in my own way now.

00:01:04.402 --> 00:01:07.319
I feel a sort of connection to him.

00:01:09.849 --> 00:01:11.176
- She wasn't afraid to dream,

00:01:11.176 --> 00:01:14.611
and she let her music speak for herself.

00:01:14.611 --> 00:01:16.159
Her music is just so soulful,

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:19.826
and it really just makes
you feel something.

00:01:24.452 --> 00:01:27.086
- It feels like she's almost
this mythological being,

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:28.708
which is why I think she
still has so much impact

00:01:28.708 --> 00:01:30.958
even years after her death.

00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:41.686
- It was really cool to like
channel my Peruvian roots.

00:01:41.686 --> 00:01:45.853
(Peruvian guitar and flute music)

00:01:48.563 --> 00:01:50.000
She has a Cinderella story.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.295
She was cleaning homes, she was
selling food on the streets,

00:01:53.295 --> 00:01:55.058
she always had her dream of singing,

00:01:55.058 --> 00:01:57.975
and she was so passionate about it.

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:04.922
- Seeing the set, and
even seeing the things,

00:02:04.922 --> 00:02:06.261
like the tools and the paint,

00:02:06.261 --> 00:02:08.452
I just was imagining, what was life like?

00:02:08.452 --> 00:02:10.872
What was it like to be this
creative person back then,

00:02:10.872 --> 00:02:15.039
and to be making political
statements through your art work.

00:02:16.198 --> 00:02:20.365
(chill Latin guitar and percussion)

00:02:29.707 --> 00:02:31.896
- I think someone like Evita

00:02:31.896 --> 00:02:34.646
is always someone to remember by.

00:02:35.584 --> 00:02:38.917
(bandonion tango music)

00:02:40.214 --> 00:02:41.537
You know, we're trying to picture her

00:02:41.537 --> 00:02:43.032
in her most passionate time,

00:02:43.032 --> 00:02:45.661
which was when she was giving
speeches to the people.

00:02:45.661 --> 00:02:47.275
She was very big with her arms,

00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:49.858
she had very big arm movements.

00:02:51.742 --> 00:02:55.075
(bandonion tango music)

00:02:56.453 --> 00:02:57.582
Really great experience, you know,

00:02:57.582 --> 00:03:00.827
remembering what she stood for
and learning more about her.

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:02.529
I'm definitely proud to be Argentinian.

00:03:02.529 --> 00:03:05.137
- It was just exciting to
shine a little bit of light

00:03:05.137 --> 00:03:07.015
on somebody who represents
my country in the best way.

00:03:07.015 --> 00:03:09.183
- She was a very proud Peruvian woman,

00:03:09.183 --> 00:03:11.638
who's killin' it, and I'm just so proud

00:03:11.638 --> 00:03:15.018
to be able to transform into her today.

00:03:15.018 --> 00:03:19.185
(Latin acoustic guitar and percussion)

